WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.520
- All right, we have just enough time

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:02.650
for one more great story.

00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:04.022
This is "What I'm Liking."

00:00:04.022 --> 00:00:06.940
(upbeat music plays)

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:08.740
- What does it take to be happy?

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.070
It is a question all of us
have asked at one point,

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.950
and something author Nataly
Kogan has tried to answer.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.900
Her new book is called "The
Awesome Human Project" and in it

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:18.880
she talks about how to
live more meaningful

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:20.170
and happier lives.

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:21.470
We have Nataly on the line right now,

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:23.590
and she watched on NBC Boston.

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:24.423
What's up, Nataly?

00:00:24.423 --> 00:00:27.366
(audience cheering and clapping)

00:00:27.366 --> 00:00:28.781
- Hi everyone! I'm so excited to be here.

00:00:28.781 --> 00:00:30.960
- [Kelly] Aw, we're so
excited to have you,

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:31.793
we're so honored to have you.

00:00:31.793 --> 00:00:33.030
You have an amazing backstory.

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:36.000
So your family came to
the US as refugees, right?

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.070
- [Nataly] We did, I grew up
in the former Soviet Union,

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:41.450
and we came with six suitcases
as refugees when I was 13,

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:45.280
and began my American dream,
in the projects on welfare,

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.260
outside of Detroit, and I
didn't speak any English,

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:50.380
we didn't have any money,
very grateful to be here,

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:52.520
and the only thing I knew
how to do was work hard,

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:56.320
so I fell into this trap
of, "I'll be happy when...",

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.130
like "I'll be happy
when I work really hard

00:00:58.130 --> 00:00:59.860
and save enough money to buy a house,

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:02.270
or, you know, get a really good job,"

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:04.220
and none of that really made me happy,

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.860
and instead, I burnt out,

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:08.670
and that's what started my
journey of figuring out,

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:11.210
what does it actually take
to live a happier life?

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:12.694
And I started a company called "Happier",

00:01:12.694 --> 00:01:15.910
and "The Awesome Human
Project" is my second book,

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:19.670
and my mission in it is
to help us all find a way

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:21.649
to live happier lives.

00:01:21.649 --> 00:01:22.897
(audience clapping)

00:01:22.897 --> 00:01:25.587
- I think a lot of
people fall prey to that,

00:01:25.587 --> 00:01:27.617
"When I achieve this,
this will make me happy",

00:01:27.617 --> 00:01:28.750
"When I do this, this will make me happy."

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:30.450
I feel like a lot of us fall prey to that.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.773
So what are some ways we can
all find deeper happiness?

00:01:32.773 --> 00:01:35.040
- Yes, it's such a great point.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.460
So, the process that I
think is the most important

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.280
to begin with is the process of gratitude,

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.380
and that's what I talk about
in my work and my book.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:45.478
The thing is, our brain is so focused

00:01:45.478 --> 00:01:47.290
on everything that's negative.

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:48.670
It's not 'cause we're negative people,

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:50.000
it's because negativity is,

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.894
usually signifies possible danger, right?

00:01:51.894 --> 00:01:55.720
So we have to practice
gratitude to remind ourselves

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:57.920
about the small, positive
things in our lives.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.750
So a process that I teach
often is to write down

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:04.420
three things you are
grateful for every morning.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:06.700
And it's something that
I love to do, and Kelly,

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:08.640
I know your show is
sponsored by Pilot Pens,

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:10.730
and I just have to tell
you, I'm a huge fan,

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:13.600
so I use Pilot Pens in
my gratitude journal

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:15.630
to write down three small things

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:17.440
that I'm grateful for every day.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:19.558
- [Kelly] I do as well.

00:02:19.558 --> 00:02:21.700
A friend told me to do
that like a year ago,

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.040
and that's what I do as well.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.840
It's very, it does
change your perspective,

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:27.230
even if you're having
like, a really hard time,

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:29.480
it makes you focus more
towards the positive.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:30.470
It's really good.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:31.947
I used to, I'm gonna be honest
with you, I used to be like

00:02:31.947 --> 00:02:33.750
(mockingly) "I'm gonna write
in my gratitude journal,

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:35.090
and I'm gonna be happier."

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:36.270
I used to make fun of that.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:38.282
And now, I'm like one of them, so.

00:02:38.282 --> 00:02:39.157
(laughter)

00:02:39.157 --> 00:02:41.381
When you can't beat 'em, join 'em.

00:02:41.381 --> 00:02:44.040
But I love it, it does work,
I'm telling you it does work.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:45.400
But thank you so much Nataly,

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:46.911
I'm liking your post right now, okay?

00:02:46.911 --> 00:02:49.337
Pick up "The Awesome Human Project"

00:02:49.337 --> 00:02:51.090
wherever you get your books from.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:52.581
We want to spread a little happiness

00:02:52.581 --> 00:02:54.080
in our audience right now,

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:56.131
so thanks to our season-long
partner Pilot Pen,

00:02:56.131 --> 00:02:58.858
makers of G2, the go-to pen
of the Kelly Clarkson show,

00:02:58.858 --> 00:03:02.080
there is a check for $1000
under someone's chair

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:03.670
in the audience, and I
don't know which one it is,

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:05.258
so look under your chair, who has it?

00:03:05.258 --> 00:03:07.980
(drumroll playing)

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:09.870
- You're not even gonna look?

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:11.387
I hope it's you.

00:03:12.409 --> 00:03:13.893
(audience laughing)

00:03:13.893 --> 00:03:14.755
(drumroll continues)

00:03:14.755 --> 00:03:15.829
- [Kelly] It's you!

00:03:15.829 --> 00:03:18.829
(audience cheering)

00:03:22.732 --> 00:03:24.296
- [Kelly] Like, I'm talking 'bout,

00:03:24.296 --> 00:03:27.160
if somebody tells me I
have $1000 under my seat,

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:30.480
I am ripping my seat
up like it's Christmas,

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:31.313
and y'all were just like,

00:03:31.313 --> 00:03:34.050
"Hm, I don't know, maybe it's here"

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:35.600
What's your name, where are you from?

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:37.160
- [Brianne] I'm from
Vancouver, Washington.

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:37.993
- [Kelly] Oh, I love Vancouver!

00:03:37.993 --> 00:03:38.826
- [Brianne] Yay!

00:03:38.826 --> 00:03:40.840
- Oh beautiful! What's your name?

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:41.830
- My name's Brianne.

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:43.570
- Love that name, it's a strong name!

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:44.403
I don't know if you've looked it up.

00:03:44.403 --> 00:03:45.493
- [Brianne] Yes.

00:03:45.493 --> 00:03:46.790
- Okay, all right.
Well, thank you so much,

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:48.312
I hope you have so much
fun with your money!

00:03:48.312 --> 00:03:49.827
- Yeah, thank you!

00:03:49.827 --> 00:03:51.274
(audience clapping and cheering)

00:03:51.274 --> 00:03:52.665
- [Kelly] Spend it on something fun!

00:03:52.665 --> 00:03:53.730
- Yes!

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:54.981
- If you gotta pay a bill,
pay a bill, but spend

00:03:54.981 --> 00:03:57.553
some of it on something
fun, just promise me that.

